WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
A new map reveals hundreds of previously unknown valleys beneath Greenland's vast ice sheet. This is Fascinating Frontiers, Episode 207. A newly developed method called ice flow perturbation analysis, produced the most detailed view yet of the bedrock beneath the Greenland ice sheet. Researchers manually mapped 1,943 subglacial valleys, about a third of these valleys are newly identified. About half of the valleys already in bed machine Greenland

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:35.680
land now extend farther inland than that earlier map indicated, in some cases by hundreds

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:36.400
of kilometers.

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:42.160
The map shows long, straight valleys in the west central region that formed well before

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:44.060
most of the ice above them existed.

00:00:44.860 --> 00:00:50.040
Scientists describe the findings in a NASA-led paper published in geophysical research letters.

00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:56.440
The Greenland ice sheet spans 1.7 million square kilometers and measures more than three

00:00:56.440 --> 00:00:58.240
kilometers thick at its maximum.

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:05.800
The new technique infers buried topography from subtle surface signatures created as ice flows over valleys and ridges.

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:12.740
Next up, astronomy picture of the day serves as an educational resource from elementary school through graduate school.

00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:18.620
Teachers have shared examples of how they incorporate the daily images into lessons across grade levels.

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:25.080
The archive supplies ready visuals for topics ranging from planetary surfaces to distant galaxies.

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:32.060
Educators report using the images to illustrate concepts in both introductory and advanced astronomy courses.

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:37.800
On a different note, the 100th anniversary of the 1920 scale of the universe debate

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:41.880
is being commemorated with historical summaries and family reflections

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:45.460
from descendants of Harlow Shapley and Hebert Daust Curtis.

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:50.300
The original debate addressed whether the universe consisted of a single galaxy

00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:52.580
or many separate island universes.

00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:57.780
modern reflections include contributions from Virginia Trimble and Deborah Shapley.

00:01:58.340 --> 00:02:03.900
The commemoration also references a follow-up 2020 debate on the search for life beyond Earth.

00:02:04.540 --> 00:02:09.480
Shifting focus, a paper in Nature Communications Physics, reports that a laboratory

00:02:09.480 --> 00:02:13.800
test led by graduate student Kaley M. Harris at Michigan State University,

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:18.220
resolved why strontium levels appeared skewed in an ancient star.

00:02:18.220 --> 00:02:25.860
The work traces how elements such as strontium form in stars and why measured abundances sometimes deviate from models.

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:32.180
The experiment recreated stellar nucleosynthesis conditions to match observed elemental ratios.

00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:39.020
The findings address long-standing discrepancies between theory and spectroscopic data from metal-pore stars.

00:02:39.020 --> 00:02:46.260
A paper in nature astrophysics led by Domenvier-Gruol at Yale concludes that the solar system's

00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:50.460
first planets formed under high temperature conditions rather than icy ones.

00:02:51.140 --> 00:02:56.120
The study examined chemical signatures preserved in meteorites to determine the thermal environment

00:02:56.120 --> 00:02:58.580
during the earliest stages of planet formation.

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:04.140
The results indicate that inner solar system materials experience temperatures high enough

00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:07.800
to vaporize water ice before planet decimals are created.

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:13.680
This thermal history contrasts with models that assumed cold, ice-rich building blocks

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:15.080
for the terrestrial planets.

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:20.260
Astronauts who previously flew around the Moon now have new assignments from NASA.

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:25.220
The agency is redirecting their experience toward upcoming exploration goals.

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:31.760
The reassignments draw on the crew members prior lunar orbital flights to support current mission planning.

00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:36.460
NASA has not released the specific roles or timelines for the new positions.

00:03:36.460 --> 00:03:37.200
observations.

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:42.300
Observations show one planet moving in the opposite direction from its host star's rotation.

00:03:43.060 --> 00:03:47.460
Researchers have not yet identified the mechanism that produced this reversed orbit.

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:52.180
The discovery adds to the small number of known retrograde exoplanets.

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:57.560
Current formation theories struggle to explain how such an orbit could arise without external

00:03:57.560 --> 00:03:58.840
gravitational influence.

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:04.980
Research suggests that caves in Australia's Nullerbore plane may preserve chemical or

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:07.720
structural evidence similar to what could exist on Mars.

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:14.260
Scientists are examining mineral formations and microbial traces inside the caves to refine

00:04:14.260 --> 00:04:16.580
search strategies for future Mars missions.

00:04:17.240 --> 00:04:22.160
The arid environment and stable geology of the Nullerbor provide a terrestrial analog

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:25.180
for potential subsurface habitats on the red planet.

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:31.320
The study focuses on secondary mineral deposits that could record past biological activity.

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:37.280
High-resolution observations with the upgraded giant meter wave radio telescope, combined

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:43.220
with Mircate and very large array data, reveal four pairs of radio structures around Galaxy

00:04:44.660 --> 00:04:49.640
J023721.13-010528.5.

00:04:50.060 --> 00:04:55.540
The central black hole appears to have launched powerful jets on four distinct occasions, making

00:04:55.540 --> 00:04:59.500
this the first confirmed example of a quadruple double radio galaxy.

00:04:59.500 --> 00:05:05.300
A paper describing the discovery was posted to the archive pre-print server on September 8.

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:12.320
The source lies at a redshift of 0.372 and is hosted by a massive electrical galaxy.

00:05:13.140 --> 00:05:18.340
NASA renewed its request for people to submit photos of the sky to help calibrate satellite

00:05:18.340 --> 00:05:20.400
instruments and study cloud patterns.

00:05:20.980 --> 00:05:24.860
Participants can upload images through the agency's Citizen Science Portal.

00:05:24.860 --> 00:05:30.000
The photographs assist in validating data from orbiting sensors that monitor atmospheric

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:30.840
conditions.

00:05:31.160 --> 00:05:35.540
The call builds on previous citizen science campaigns that have collected thousands

00:05:35.540 --> 00:05:36.460
of sky images.

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:42.580
Observations from South Africa's Miracat radio telescope have produced signals consistent

00:05:42.580 --> 00:05:44.200
with an exoplanet candidate.

00:05:44.900 --> 00:05:47.740
Further analysis is underway to confirm the detection.

00:05:48.460 --> 00:05:52.920
The telescope's high sensitivity to radio wavelengths allows it to search for planetary

00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:58.000
signatures that optical instruments might miss. Confirmation would add to the growing

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:03.760
catalog of worlds discovered by radio methods. A published letter argues that data centers

00:06:03.760 --> 00:06:09.220
and spaceports could position Louisiana as a leader in space exploration while rebuilding

00:06:09.220 --> 00:06:15.120
coastal areas and retaining skilled workers. The author contrasts this vision with the state's

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:20.280
current challenges of population loss and land erosion. The letter notes that existing

00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:24.740
industries have already produced thousands of workers with relevant technical skills.

00:06:25.340 --> 00:06:30.000
It calls for leadership that balances economic development with environmental protection.

00:06:30.880 --> 00:06:36.140
The new ice flow perturbation analysis technique detects faint surface signatures left by

00:06:36.140 --> 00:06:37.580
buried valleys and ridges.

00:06:38.320 --> 00:06:43.460
Researchers use these signatures to trace valleys that previous maps had left fragmented or

00:06:43.460 --> 00:06:44.360
missed entirely.

00:06:45.080 --> 00:06:50.180
Extending some valleys hundreds of kilometers farther inland changes how models reconstruct

00:06:50.180 --> 00:06:55.980
the island's pre-glacial landscape. The updated topography will feed into future versions of

00:06:55.980 --> 00:07:01.060
bed machine Greenland and improve projections of how the ice sheet may respond to warming.

00:07:01.980 --> 00:07:06.060
Imagine the Greenland ice sheet as a layer of frosting three kilometers deep,

00:07:06.560 --> 00:07:10.340
spread across an area larger than the distance from Vancouver to Halifax.

00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:15.940
Beneath that frosting lies a network of valleys that formed when the rock was still exposed to

00:07:15.940 --> 00:07:18.860
rivers and glaciers long before the ice arrived.

00:07:19.580 --> 00:07:23.940
Satellites measure tiny bumps and dips on the ice surface that trace the shape of those

00:07:23.940 --> 00:07:28.080
hidden valleys because flowing ice bends slightly over ridges and dips.

00:07:28.820 --> 00:07:36.420
Researchers manually traced 1,943 such valleys, finding that roughly 650 of them had never

00:07:36.420 --> 00:07:37.460
been recorded before.

00:07:38.180 --> 00:07:43.180
The longest newly extended segments reach hundreds of kilometers further inland than earlier

00:07:43.180 --> 00:07:43.860
maps showed.

00:07:43.860 --> 00:07:49.540
We can now see the ancient river and glacial channels that predate the ice sheet, yet no

00:07:49.540 --> 00:07:54.880
one knows exactly how much of that older topography still steers the ice flow today, or how

00:07:54.880 --> 00:07:57.660
quickly those channels might change if the ice thins.

00:07:58.280 --> 00:08:03.580
Before we go, keep an eye on confirmation efforts for the Miracat exoplanet candidate

00:08:03.580 --> 00:08:05.380
as additional data come in.

